\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{Depth-graduated curation and deployment.}
Predicted depth can serve as a quality proxy for importance weighting \cite{dsir2023,qurating2024}, down-weighting deeply recursive samples that binary filters \cite{drayson2025_detection_prevents_collapse} would indiscriminately discard.
Cross-strategy deployment requires addressing the temperature-induced d1/d2 inversion (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}): per-strategy normalization partially corrects the scale component (GPT-2~XL d1v2 AUC $0.24{\to}0.56$; OLMo $0.19{\to}0.70$) but leaves a residual gap to within-strategy performance (${\sim}0.83$), motivating a two-stage pipeline of strategy identification followed by strategy-conditioned classification.

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether the atlas results (69.6--85.4\%) reflect genuine statistical regularities or training artifacts, evaluating four axes: classifier choice (\S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}), cross-model transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossmodel}), cross-strategy transfer (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}), and end-to-end learning (\S\ref{sec:gen:e2e}).

\subsection{Baseline Comparison}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares classification methods on the Pythia-1.4B nucleus setting.
Three feature-based methods---Random Forest (RF), CORAL ordinal regression \cite{coral2020}, and DivEye \cite{diveye2026}---all operate on the same 19 statistical features and achieve statistically indistinguishable accuracy (78.1--78.2\%; pairwise bootstrap $p>0.05$ after Bonferroni correction, 95\% CI within $\pm$1.1pp).
These three significantly outperform CE-MLP ($p<0.001$, $+$3.8pp), which in turn trails logistic regression by $\sim$0.1pp.
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%), confirming that depth estimation requires the multi-dimensional feature representation defined in \S\ref{sec:method}.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Comparison of classification methods on Pythia-1.4B nucleus. RF, CORAL, and DivEye use the same 19 statistical features and are statistically indistinguishable (bootstrap $p>0.05$, Bonferroni-corrected). Chance = 25\%. 5-fold CV std: 0.2--0.9pp across all methods.}
\label{tab:baselines}
\begin{tabular}{lc}
\toprule
Method & 4-class Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
RF (19 features) & 78.2 \\
CORAL & 78.1 \\
DivEye & 78.2 \\
CE-MLP & 74.9 \\
LR & 75.0 \\
DeepSeek V3.2 (zero-shot)\footnotemark & 40.0 \\
ppl-regression (single feature) & 25.1 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\footnotetext{Evaluated on 200 samples (approximately 50 per class) from the Pythia-1.4B nucleus cell only, due to API cost; 95\% CI: $\pm$6.8pp. All other methods use the full 20{,}000-sample set.}
\end{table}

That three structurally different classifiers achieve identical performance confirms that the discriminative signal resides in the 19 features, not the classifier architecture; a zero-shot LLM baseline (DeepSeek V3.2) reaches only 40.0\%.
Figure~\ref{fig:confusion_matrix} shows d0 is near-perfectly identified in both settings; errors concentrate at the d2/d3 boundary in-distribution and spread across d1--d3 under cross-model transfer.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig_confusion_matrix.pdf}
\caption{Confusion matrices for the best in-distribution setting (GPT-2 XL, temp $\tau{=}0.9$, 85.4\%) and the best cross-model leave-one-out transfer (OLMo holdout, 69.2\%). d0 recall exceeds 99\% in both; errors spread across the d1--d3 range under transfer.}
\label{fig:confusion_matrix}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Cross-Model Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossmodel}

We perform leave-one-model-family-out evaluation: train on two families (nucleus only), test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_model}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-model transfer (leave-one-out, nucleus only). Train on 2 model families, test on held-out 3rd. Chance = 25\%.}
\label{tab:cross_model}
\begin{tabular}{lccccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Acc & d0 & d1 & d2 & d3 \\
 & (\%) & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. & Rec. \\
\midrule
Pythia & 62.4 & 100.0 & 49.3 & 26.8 & 73.6 \\
OLMo & \textbf{69.2} & 99.3 & 75.5 & 43.3 & 58.8 \\
GPT-2 XL & 67.6 & 98.5 & 74.7 & 52.6 & 44.5 \\
\midrule
Mean & 66.4 & 99.3 & 66.5 & 40.9 & 59.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean transfer accuracy reaches 66.4\% (2.7$\times$ chance); d0 recall ($>$98\%) inflates the headline while d2 drops to 26.8\% (Pythia holdout).

\subsection{Cross-Strategy Transfer}
\label{sec:gen:crossstrategy}

We train on two decoding strategies and test on the held-out third (Table~\ref{tab:cross_strategy}).

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\small
\caption{Cross-strategy transfer (leave-one-out). Train on 2 strategies, test on held-out 3rd. Chance = 25\%.}
\label{tab:cross_strategy}
\begin{tabular}{lccc}
\toprule
Holdout & Pythia & OLMo & GPT-2 XL \\
 & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) & Acc (\%) \\
\midrule
nucleus & \textbf{69.4} & \textbf{65.4} & \textbf{59.9} \\
temp ($\tau{=}0.9$) & 62.8 & 58.2 & 56.1 \\
top-$k$ ($k{=}50$) & 51.5 & 54.9 & 53.6 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Mean cross-strategy accuracy (59.1\%) falls 7pp below cross-model transfer (66.4\%), indicating that strategy-induced shifts pose a greater challenge than architectural differences.
As with cross-model transfer, d0 recall remains high ($\geq$89.5\%), inflating headline accuracy; d2 recall ranges from 3.7\% to 19\%, confirming mid-chain depths as the primary confusion locus.

\paragraph{Temperature-induced boundary inversion.}
Training on nucleus + top-$k$ and testing on temperature yields d1v2 AUC below 0.5 (GPT-2~XL: 0.129, OLMo: 0.253), inverting the d1/d2 boundary (\S\ref{sec:atlas:strategy}).
Pooled standardization \cite{sugiyama2007_importance_weighting} fails (d1v2 AUC $\leq$0.24), but per-strategy z-score normalization partially recovers the boundary (GPT-2~XL $0.24{\to}0.56$, OLMo $0.19{\to}0.70$, Pythia $0.52{\to}0.72$), leaving a residual gap to within-strategy performance (${\sim}$0.83).

\subsection{End-to-End Learning}
\label{sec:gen:e2e}

DeBERTa-v3-base \cite{he2021_deberta_v3} and RoBERTa-base \cite{liu2019_roberta} finetuned end-to-end both converge to ${\sim}$59\% (19pp below RF 78.2\%; 1.2pp inter-backbone gap rules out architecture bias).
Linear probing shows PLMs cannot recover the dominant depth features: \textsc{var\_surprisal} and \textsc{p90\_ppl} yield negative $R^2$ from DeBERTa hidden states (Appendix~\ref{app:feature_overlap}), yet SHAP analysis attributes 88.8\% of RF's contribution to perplexity/surprisal features.
DCOL \cite{cloc2025} reached only 64.5\% (Appendix~\ref{app:dcol_iterations}).
All end-to-end approaches (58--65\%) substantially underperform feature-based methods, confirming that depth estimation is substantially enhanced by explicit distributional statistics.

\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

We formalized recursive generation depth estimation and showed that 19 statistical features achieve 70--85\% four-class accuracy ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}{=}4$; cross-model 66.4\%, cross-strategy 59.1\%).
Future work should address wild-data transfer, larger scales, and strategy-conditioned classifiers.
Code and data: released upon acceptance.

\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Cross-strategy deployment constraint.}
Cross-strategy transfer induces a d1/d2 boundary inversion under temperature sampling.
Per-strategy z-score normalization partially corrects the inversion (GPT-2~XL d1v2 AUC $0.24{\to}0.56$, OLMo $0.19{\to}0.70$) but leaves a residual gap relative to within-strategy performance (${\sim}0.83$), indicating that a two-stage pipeline (strategy identification + strategy-conditioned classification) is needed for reliable cross-strategy deployment.

\paragraph{d0 inflation of headline metrics.}
Depth~0 (human-written text) is near-perfectly separable (recall $>$98\% across all settings), inflating headline accuracy.
Cross-model transfer accuracy of 66.4\% is substantially driven by d0; d2 recall drops to 26.8\% in the worst case (Pythia holdout).

\paragraph{Controlled vs.\ ecological setting.}
All experiments use Wikipedia seed text with controlled recursive generation (LoRA fine-tuning, 1--1.5B parameter models).
Transfer to mixed-domain corpora, instruction-tuned models, and larger scales (7B+) remains open.
Wild-data calibration is probed only indirectly; ecological transfer is future work.

\paragraph{EDD monotonicity assumption.}
Our EDD definition (Eq.~\ref{eq:edd}) requires all pairwise AUCs up to depth $d^*$ to exceed $T$, which is well-behaved when AUC decays monotonically---as observed in all our configurations.
However, \citet{bakshi2026_collapse_improvement} show that early recursion can temporarily \emph{improve} model quality, which could produce non-monotonic AUC profiles (e.g., a dip at d1/d2 followed by recovery at d2/d3).
In such cases EDD would conservatively report a lower value than the deepest discriminable pair, potentially underestimating the true discriminable range; extending EDD to handle non-contiguous discriminable regions is future work.
The choice of threshold $T$ is also application-dependent: at $T{=}0.80$, several cells lose one depth level relative to $T{=}0.75$.

\paragraph{Reference model dependency.}
Surprisal-based features are computed relative to GPT-2 base (124M), introducing partial overlap when GPT-2~XL is held out, though GPT-2~XL holdout accuracy (67.6\%) does not exceed the unrelated OLMo (69.2\%).
The choice of reference model affects feature values; a different reference model may shift absolute feature magnitudes and potentially alter discriminability at depth boundaries.

\paragraph{Additional caveats.}
Detection features degrade under domain shift \cite{pudasaini2026_detection_fails}; our depth features are likely susceptible.
\citet{falahati2026_curated} show that curated synthetic data need not trigger collapse, attenuating the fingerprints we exploit.
